  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nStorms developed prior to midnight over the Flint Hills in an \narea of increasing 850-700mb theta-e advection. Storms look to \nbe rooted somewhere in the 800-700mb ranged which would give \nthem about 2,000J/kg of CAPE to work with and around 30-35kts of\neffective shear. So some hail in the dime to quarter size and \nheavy rain look to be the main threats this morning. \nShowers/storms will linger through at least 15z over the Flint \nHills/se KS and will start to move out of the forecast area in \nthe 15-16z time frame. Even with a boundary over southern KS\nthis afternoon, once storms push south, no additional \nconvection is anticipated for the remainder of the day.\n\nAs shortwave trough tracks from the Upper Mississippi Valley \ninto the Great Lakes region, the upper ridge centered over the \nFour Corners region will start to slowly build east to start the\nweekend. This will allow a warming trend to commence as much \nwarmer 850mb temps start to creep into the forecast area. While \na few locations west of I-135 will likely hit 100 on Sat, most \nof the area is expected to hit the century mark on Sun. Not much\nis expected to change through Tue, as the upper ridge remains \nfrom the Southern Plains through the Central Great Basin. This \nwill continue to promote highs in the 100-105 range for much of \nthe forecast area. If there's any positive with this stretch of \nextreme heat, it's that dewpoints are expected to generally be \nin the upper 50s and low 60s when our max temps occur. This will\nkeep the heat indices from getting too out of control. \n\nThere is decent model agreement that we will get back into more of a \nnorthwest flow regime for Wed into Thu as a series of upper impulses \ntrack from MB/SK and down into the Great Lakes region. This will \nallow a boundary to sink down into the Plains, increasing storm \nchances and bringing some relief from the extreme heat.",
